{"gid":"2026_05_PHI_JAX","week":5,"away":"PHI","home":"JAX","away_col":"#3FB7B0","home_col":"#19D3C5","spread":-2.5,"total":43.5,"fav":"PHI","hpts":20,"apts":23,"kickoff":"Sun Oct 11 \u00b7 09:30","roof":"Outdoors","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>DeVonta Smith</strong> draws a plus matchup \u2014 JAX concede <strong>68 yds/game to WR1s</strong> (league ~58), and PHI funnel 25% of targets his way. A rec-yards spot.</p><p>The <strong>JAX</strong> welcome the <strong>PHI</strong> as underdogs of <strong>2.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>43.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>PHI 23\u201320</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>26% of its snaps with PHI in front</strong>, <strong>39% within one score</strong>, and <strong>34% with PHI chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>This is a <strong>coin-flip script</strong>. With only 2.5 points separating them, neither team projects to spend much time in a blowout state \u2014 most of the game lives in the 'close' bucket, where play-calling stays balanced and usage tracks each player's established role rather than being distorted by score. In tight games the stars on both sides see their normal, dependable workloads, and there is less garbage-time leakage to backups.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p>Tough sledding for <strong>Saquon Barkley</strong> \u2014 JAX are stingy against the run (109 yds/game, well below the ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p>Tough sledding for <strong>Chris Rodriguez Jr.</strong> \u2014 PHI are stingy against the run (112 yds/game, well below the ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p><strong>Jalen Hurts</strong> has a passing-yards tailwind \u2014 JAX allow <strong>252 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p>Sneaky value on <strong>Dontayvion Wicks</strong> (WR2) \u2014 JAX leak 44 yds/game to the WR2 role, above the ~36 norm.</p><p>Sneaky value on <strong>Parker Washington</strong> (WR2) \u2014 PHI leak 42 yds/game to the WR2 role, above the ~36 norm.</p><p><strong>Parker Washington</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 PHI hold WR1s to just 52 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Saquon Barkley</strong> is a bell-cow (77% of carries) meeting a defense that allows 0.49 RB rushing TDs/game \u2014 strong anytime-TD equity.</p><p><strong>Dallas Goedert</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 JAX are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.31 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p>PHI pound it at the goal line (36% pass in the red zone) \u2014 <strong>Saquon Barkley</strong> owns the rushing-TD equity.</p><h3>PHI \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>PHI are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>37%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p><strong>Saquon Barkley</strong> (15 carries, 70 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>DeVonta Smith</strong> projects for <strong>6 targets and 54 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Dallas Goedert</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 51 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>JAX \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>JAX are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (43% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Chris Rodriguez Jr.</strong> (10 carries, 49 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Parker Washington</strong> projects for <strong>7 targets and 54 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Brenton Strange</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 36 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 2-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"PHI","col":"#3FB7B0","players":[{"name":"Jalen Hurts","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":221.09,"book":null,"over_th":207.76,"under_th":230.09,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.34,"book":null,"over_th":0.99,"under_th":1.37,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false}],"td":{"pct":31.5,"fair":"9/4","book":null,"value":null}},{"name":"Saquon Bar